Affirmation/negation pair on the Collatz CORE (descent), the non-forking razor. Read the two against
each other. This is the same test just run on RH (rh_frob_explicit_affirm / rh_frob_implicit_negate),
where the negation proved NON-INSTANTIABLE: both framings imscribed to byte-identical tuples at the
descent crystal 3442270, both held with μ∘δ=id, the Grammar refused to instantiate RH's failure.

Collatz reduction (p4rakernel/p4ramill/.../Erdos/CollatzDepthSplit.lean, 260 thms *sans* sorry): shortcut
map col n = if even n then n/2 else (3n+1)/2; descent = ∀n>1 ∃k col^[k]n<n; Collatz ⟺ descent ⟺ no
non-descender ⟺ no divergence ∧ no nontrivial cycle. The descent is the Frobenius reconnection
(μ∘δ=id): the halving/tripling split fuses back. Descent crystal = 3442270 (⊙=𐑻 exceptional point) =
the SAME crystal as RH's core and riemann_critical_line — the shared address [[conjectures_are_povms]].

Test prediction: the negation (a non-descender: divergent or cyclic orbit, split/merge NOT composing to
identity) should be NON-INSTANTIABLE — imscribe to the same tuple/crystal as the affirmation, both hold,
both μ∘δ=id. The Grammar cannot build the absence of the reconnection; the wall is not constructible
(law_no_cant). If so, Collatz's core is closed at the fundamental (imscription) level, same as RH — and
they are one facet of the dual-frame SIC-POVM read at the same crystal, not two conjectures.

Register semantics: holds() = exposed empty ∧ live_clears>0; vacuous() = exposed empty ∧ live_clears==0;
leak = cleared with nothing banked; a count banked in an unfused frame is HELD; restored via ∋ fuse =
μ∘δ=id reconnection. Four-valued register (T closes, F ill-typed, N never forks, B both/barrier) is the
holder opened first, held through.
